Z||000005||BOILERMAKER||07-02-2000||01:18 AM||||I take it that you have never had a typo before, huh?  Take it easy, man, if you don't want his advise, then just say so.||||reg||1
Z||000006||86SE||07-02-2000||02:09 AM||||My guess would be the ignition coil, happen to me at school, same exact thing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||07-02-2000||03:24 AM||||Boiler:  No, it sounded rude the way he made that comment.  Maybe it was a joke, but I didn't post a topic to have someone say:<P>"Stuff happens."<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||07-02-2000||11:50 AM||||It is definately NOT a case of STUFF HAPPENS!!!! What you have is a SERIOUS PROBLEM that will REQUIRE dropping the FUEL TANK!!!!! Under no circumstances should you drive your car UNTIL YOU DROP YOUR FUEL TANK AND REMOVE YOUR FUEL PUMP!!!!!!!  Trust me, this is no simple GLITCH caused by dirty contacts in the 16 year old ignition switch- that would be EASY!!!<P>BTW- the Gremlin is a 27 year old car. Deal with it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Homicidal||07-02-2000||12:22 PM||||I smell something Stimpy........coming from your direction..............................<BR>smells like......................smells like....................some bull$hit........mixed with TONS of sarcasm!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||07-02-2000||12:47 PM||||Ya caught me! I guess it's cabin fever.  Here it is in July and it has been pouring down rain all day. My Fiero damn near started floating when I went out this morning. <P>Asfar as the thing with your car, it sounds like vapor lock or a bad thing in the ignition system that doesn't like heat.||||reg||1
Z||000011||ka4nkf||07-04-2000||08:48 AM||||I would say say she was listening to a bad tune on the radio.||||reg||1
Z||000012||RLMay||07-04-2000||10:56 AM||||I agree with Stimpy.  My 86Gt exhibited the same sympton.  When I would attempt to restart the engine warm, not necessarily hot, it wouldn't start.  After it cooled completely, it would start.  It turned out to be the fuel pump.  There is a schrader valve on the fuel rail, generally near the thermostat.  Test with a fuel testing pressure guage,  Mine was about 10 psi, should be about 40 psi if I rember correctly.  I would test the presure first, as it is some amount of work to remove the fuel tank and pump.||||reg||1
Z||000013||stimpy||07-04-2000||12:14 PM||||HAH! Told ya so!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||fldevel||07-04-2000||12:42 PM||||i also have to agree with stimpy had yet the same problem myself with my 85GT i just ignored it till one day the car died in the middle of the highway and was forced to replace the fuel pump ,also u will notice a large lack of power just before the fuel pump goes.. good luck ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Homicidal||07-04-2000||01:17 PM||||Hey, if it's true, I am glad I know now!  At least I can stop a problem before it becomes worse! <P>Or just start saving!||||reg||1
Z||000016||stimpy||07-04-2000||03:36 PM||||And ya wouldn't believe me...<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Cliff Pennock||07-04-2000||04:22 PM||||If you have an automatic, then it also could be the Park-switch that's just a little bit dirty. Next time it happens (if it ever happens again), try fiddling with the stick a bit.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||fieroshop||07-05-2000||10:25 AM||||Experience has it that you have one of two problems. The first and I would check this out is the ignition module. When the engine is hot the car won't start indicates the module starting to go. This will accur more times and in the end the car won't start leaving you stranded. The second and not as easily diagnosed is the fule pump unless you have a test gauge measuring the fuel pressure at the shrader valve. If pressure is under thiry pounds then you will have to replace the fuel pump which is a pain the a__.||||reg||1
Z||000019||BellaFiero||07-05-2000||11:16 AM||||I had this problem with my '88 automatic. When I got home from work, if I tried to go out that night in the car ( leaving less than two hours from when I got home ), she'd refuse to start. Would crank and crank but wouldn't catch... but come morning, when the engine was cold, I'd drive to work, and everything would be fine. Start up without a problem. So we dropped the fuel and swapped out the fuel pump. Now, some people say it was a real hassle. I don't think so. Time consuming maybe, but not too difficult. The fuel tank looks like a small torpedo, and the fuel pump sits right at the top. Make sure, if you do this, that you get a new fuel sock and gaskets... might as well do it now. Preventive maintenance is a beautiful thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
